1
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:05,120
At this point we have seen that 
the critical section problem can  

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:08,480
be solved in several different 
ways. We are going to look into  

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:13,360
one more solution which has some extra nice 
property: the Lamport's Bakery algorithm.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:18,160
Lamport is one of the recipients of the Turing 
award and came up with the following idea.  

5
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:24,160
Simply put: all the threads who want to enter a 
critical section need to get a number. The thread  

6
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:30,080
with the smallest number is the one that can 
enter. Imagine a take-a-number system, basically.

7
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:34,160
We assume the numbers given by the 
take-a-number system are non-decreasing.  

8
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:37,760
Non-decreasing means that two or more 
consecutive numbers can be the same.  

9
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:43,200
If we also assume each thread has a unique 
id, we can break ties saying that if two or  

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:48,400
more threads have the same number, the one with 
the smallest id can enter its critical section.

11
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:54,720
Let's look at the complete example.
We have two shared arrays each with n  

12
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:59,120
elements (n is the number of threads).
The first array, choosing, contains  

13
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,880
booleans initialized to false.
The second array, number,  

14
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:09,280
contains integers initialized to 0.
In its entry section, thread i starts  

15
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:15,360
by setting true to its entry in choosing.
Then, it computes the max value for all entries in  

16
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:22,240
number plus 1 and stores it in its number entry.
Finally, it sets false to its entry in choosing. 

17
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:29,280
Then, it does a complete pass over the 
n entries of the arrays, but it spins on  

18
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:36,800
each entry j if such an entry is choosing or if 
such an entry has a lower number,j combination  

19
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:42,080
but the number is not 0 (number,j combination 
because we use threads' ids to break ties). 

20
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:47,760
Then it enters the section.
Once the critical section is over,  

21
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:53,920
the thread simply sets its number back to 0.
Before discussing why this solution is valid,  

22
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:58,400
please notice that each thread is only 
modifying its own entry in the array.

23
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:03,600
I ask you to formally reason 
about why this solution is valid,  

24
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:09,440
I am only doing a rapid discussion. For mutual 
exclusion, you can conclude that if a thread  

25
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:14,800
enters its critical section, then that thread 
is sure no other thread has a lower number.  

26
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:21,200
When the thread is in, can another thread obtain 
a lower number and enter the critical section?  

27
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,480
No, beacuse the number is always 
the max of all threads + 1,  

28
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:30,000
so it will be at least +1 compared to the 
thread that is running its critical section. 

29
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:34,560
When it comes to progress, the thread with 
the smallest number can always proceed. 

30
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:40,320
When it comes to fairness, notice that once a 
thread starts waiting and another enters its  

31
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:45,920
critical section, the next number the latter will 
get is higher than that of the waiting thread.

32
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,360
Now going back to what I said in the beginning, 
about the nice property of this algorithm,  

33
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:58,160
is that it works also with primitives that 
are weaker than atomic read/write. In fact,  

34
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:03,840
this is one of the algorithms that gave rise 
to research in lock-free synchronization.

